WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.334
Hi, I'm Pretzel!

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:05.813
This repo races two decision models through Chrome's dinosaur game.

00:00:05.813 --> 00:00:09.292
Here's how the harness works, and what it really measures.

00:00:10.517 --> 00:00:14.219
The harness describes the world as it will be when each answer lands.

00:00:14.219 --> 00:00:16.498
So a slower model gets a different question.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.384
Start with the clock.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:26.447
One round trip to Jev's hosted API takes about three hundred and seventy milliseconds.

00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:28.956
A frame lasts sixteen point six seven.

00:00:29.719 --> 00:00:33.087
So one answer costs twenty-two frames: about the whole window in

00:00:33.087 --> 00:00:34.312
which a jump works.

00:00:35.717 --> 00:00:40.561
Each move, a planner simulates the physics ahead, using this player's own measured latency.

00:00:40.981 --> 00:00:45.353
It writes the question: jump, duck or run, each labelled best, safe or unsafe.

00:00:45.773 --> 00:00:47.455
The model answers with probabilities.

00:00:47.455 --> 00:00:51.491
Several questions stay in flight, so even the slow player gets a

00:00:51.491 --> 00:00:52.837
turn every six frames.

00:00:53.257 --> 00:00:57.294
The pilot throws away answers whose premise went stale, and plays the rest.

00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:02.350
On one frame, Laya is told the cacti are a hundred and

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.872
seventy-two pixels away, and that waiting is best.

00:01:05.292 --> 00:01:08.352
Jev is told seventy pixels, and that jumping is best.

00:01:08.352 --> 00:01:11.717
Each prompt is right about the world its answer lands in.

00:01:12.883 --> 00:01:16.748
Over the repo's own snapshots, the two get the same question only

00:01:16.748 --> 00:01:19.003
thirteen point nine percent of the time.

00:01:19.003 --> 00:01:21.258
Jev's are more forced, and so easier.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:26.142
A prompt adapted to each caller makes the score caller-dependent.

00:01:26.142 --> 00:01:28.796
To compare models, ask them identical questions.

00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:34.447
To recap: latency shapes the prompt, the prompt shapes the score, so

00:01:34.447 --> 00:01:36.272
compare models on identical questions.

00:01:36.692 --> 00:01:38.846
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:38.846 --> 00:01:39.462
I'm Pretzel.

00:01:39.462 --> 00:01:39.770
Bye!
